Each method definition advances a global world counter, and running code sees the world it started in. A function that defines a method with `@eval` and then calls it in the same running call cannot see the new method:

```julia
function run_it()
    @eval newf() = 42
    return newf()      # MethodError: method too new to be called
end
function run_ok()
    @eval newg() = 43
    return Base.invokelatest(newg)
end
run_ok()
```

`invokelatest` calls with the newest world. The first function raises a `MethodError` whose text says the function exists but no method is defined for this combination (on 1.13: "method too new to be called"). At top level, each new statement starts in the latest world, so this mostly shows up in code that defines and calls in one function body.

The caller is running in an older world than the definition. Call through `Base.invokelatest`:

```julia
function build_and_call(k)
    name = Symbol("gen_", k)
    @eval $name() = $k * 2
    return Base.invokelatest(getfield(@__MODULE__, name))
end
build_and_call(3)
```

`invokelatest` carries a dynamic-dispatch cost, so use it at the boundary where generated code is called, not in an inner loop. Better still, avoid generating functions: close over the value, `gen = () -> k * 2`.

